  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Number of analyses of the socioeconomic impacts of COVID-19 in Angola;
Level of Policy Options for Government intervention to address economic and social impact implemented; 
Number of  national employment and poverty programmes redesigned; 
Level of socio - economic Impact Analysis and recovery roadmap developed;
Number of support provided  to mitigate gender impact of the pandemic;
Number Assess and address challenges of the 1.1 million people severely affected by impact of El
Nino and climate change.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2020">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">No socio-economic assessment for COVID-19.</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Reach 450,000 people with COVID awareness and information through ongoing outreach and training activities in environmental projects at no extra cost to projects, including through the use of local languages and focusing on drought and flood affected south of the country;
2. Analyses of the socioeconomic impacts of COVID-19 in Angola published;
3.National employment and poverty programmes redesigned; 
4.Support provided to mitigate gender impact of the pandemic provided;
5.Assess and address challenges of the 1.1 million people severely affected by impact of El
Nino and climate change implemented;</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.Over 336.500 people reached by Emission of three radio programs per week in Otchivambo and Nhaneca-Humbe on climate change adaptation and disaster resilience through Radio Cunene, covering COVID aspects;
2.UNDP has technically led and conducted three socioeconomic assessments of the potential impacts of COVID-19 in Angola, including an additional assessment in partnership with UNCT and World Bank that contributed to elaborate the UN Socio-Economic Response and Recovery Plan.
3.Two MoUs signed with the Ministry of High Education and Innovation and Provincial Government to conduct the mapping of national entrepreneurship and innovation ecosystem in Angola.
4.Provided legal aid to over 600 women survivors of domestic violence. 
5.UNDP provided support to provincial coordination, communication with Huila, Namibe and Cunene Covid-19 contingency plan was produced and established partnership with provincial vice-governors leading the DRM and Provincial Civil Protection Commissions. Governments, UNDP and EU developed the COVID-19 Monitor using Power BI and GIS Map.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.	Continued support to formalization of informal operators and infrastructure improvement and facilitation access to basic services.
2.	Conduct a joint study UNDP, ILO and National Institute for Statistics of socio-economic impact of COVID on informal Economy and MSMEs
3. Mobilize and train 1000 women informal workers on the benefits of formalizing their businesses, and receive capacitation in business management and financial literacy.
4. Train 495 Agents to found a Cooperative of Rural Agents, to support 234 smallholder farmers in 4 provinces of Angola.
5. Support INAPEM in the Development of an Online Crowdfunding platform for Startups and SMEs.
6. Support INAPEM in the development of a new digital platform for the Certification of MSMEs.
7. Support INAPEM in the implementation of a pilot project o support 6 Cooperatives with production flow and access to markets.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.R: 9.848 women informal workers mobilized in 5 informal markets in Luanda
4.R: 1 Cooperative of Rural Agent Created, 123 Agents trained and 160 smallholders farmers assisted.
5.R: 25% Execution rate achieved in the development of the Online Crowdfunding platform.
6.R: A new Online Certification Platform developed for Certification of MSMEs in Angola.
7.R: Kick-Off of the activities of the project to support 6 cooperatives in Benguela.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provide technical advisory services to the Government for an assessment of social protection needs and extension of social protection to informal workers with priority to women and youth in urban areas
Support women, youth and members of key and vulnerable populations - with informal employment to register their businesses.
In partnership with INEFOP, Civil Society Organizations and business associations, support 400 women, young informal workers and members of key and vulnerable populations, who completed a vocational training course, to join an apprenticeship program in private and public companies and receive professional kits and tutoring.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Two MoUs were signed with the Ministry of High Education and Innovation and Provincial Government to conduct the mapping of national entrepreneurship and innovation ecosystem in Angola which will lead to the operationalizing of the Luanda Entrepreneurship Academic and establishment of the two Business Incubator Centers in two public universities.
UNPD has reached the initial agreement with Ministry of Economy and Planinning to support the operationalization of the National Plan for formulazation of Informal Economy Agents.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
